        <div id="movement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Movement Ratings Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most useful number on an exterior window sealant is its movement rating. It tells you how far the joint can open and close, as a percentage of its width, without the sealant tearing or losing adhesion. A sealant rated plus or minus 50 percent can handle a joint that stretches and compresses by half its width; a sealant rated plus or minus 25 percent can handle a quarter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Window joints move more than most people expect. Vinyl frames in particular expand and contract noticeably with temperature, and the siding around them moves on its own schedule. A sunny south-facing window can see large daily swings. The higher the movement rating, the more headroom the sealant has before it fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            OSI Quad Max and GE Paintable Silicone Supreme Window and Door sit at the top here, with plus or minus 50 percent from OSI's ASTM C719 testing and a Class 50 listing on retailer pages for GE. Loctite PL Window, Door and Siding is rated plus or minus 25 percent by Henkel, which is typical for polyurethane and fine for most window openings, especially masonry ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            Elongation is a different figure and is sometimes quoted alongside movement. It measures how far a sample stretches before breaking, and the numbers are much larger: OSI lists 458 percent for Quad Max clear, and Sashco quotes 700 percent or more for Lexel. Elongation is a useful indication of flexibility, but movement rating is the number designed to predict real-world joint performance, so compare products on that first.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your current window caulk keeps cracking at the same joints, a low movement rating is often the reason. Our guide to <a href="/why-does-exterior-caulk-keep-cracking/">why exterior caulk keeps cracking</a> covers the other common causes, from bead shape to poor prep.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Caulking Windows in Cold Weather
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drafty windows get noticed in autumn and winter, which is exactly when most water-based caulks cannot be applied. Application range is therefore a real buying criterion for window sealant, not a footnote. Several picks here can go on well below freezing; others need a mild day.
          </p>

          <p>
            OSI Quad Max is listed for application from 0 to 140 degrees, and Sashco Lexel from 0 to 120 degrees surface temperature. GE lists 0 to 140 degrees for its Paintable Silicone Supreme Window and Door, though it wants the sealant itself kept between 41 and 104 degrees. Retailer tables give Loctite PL Window, Door and Siding a range of 20 to 120 degrees.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based products are more restrictive. DAP lists Dynaflex Ultra for 40 to 100 degrees and advises against applying it if freezing temperatures are forecast within 24 hours. DAP Alex Fast Dry should go on above 40 degrees. If you are caulking in November, pick from the low-temperature group.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold also changes the way any sealant handles. Polymer sealants thicken and become hard to gun, which is why OSI recommends storing cartridges at room temperature for 24 hours before cold-weather use. Keeping the cartridge in a warm car or a bucket of warm water until you need it makes a real difference, and so does a high-thrust caulk gun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that cure slows in the cold. A sealant that is paint-ready in an hour at 70 degrees can stay soft much longer at 30. Plan exterior window work so the sealant has a dry stretch after application, and keep sprinklers and pressure washers away from fresh beads.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="paint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Painting Window Caulk
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your windows and trim are painted, the sealant has to accept paint, and how quickly it does so affects how you plan the job. DAP Alex Fast Dry is the fastest here, with DAP saying it can be brush-painted in about 20 minutes or spray-painted right away. That makes it ideal for interior trim, where you can caulk and paint in a single session.
          </p>

          <p>
            Among the exterior sealants, OSI markets Quad Max as paintable with latex in about an hour, and DAP lists Dynaflex Ultra as paint-ready in about an hour. GE's Paintable Silicone Supreme accepts paint, unlike standard silicone, but its long tack-free time means waiting longer before painting. Sashco says Lexel can take latex paint after 24 to 48 hours and oil-based paint only after three to four weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Loctite PL Window, Door and Siding is paintable only after full cure, which retail listings put at about seven days. That is fine for a window set in brick that will not be painted, and a nuisance on a painted wood window you want to finish in a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two general rules apply. First, use a flexible latex paint over a flexible sealant; rigid paints can crack as the bead moves. Second, do not paint a sealant before it has at least skinned over and firmed, or you will drag the surface and trap solvents underneath. When in doubt, follow the longer of the maker's paint-ready time and what the bead actually feels like.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-caulk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where Not to Caulk a Window
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every gap around a window should be sealed, and caulking the wrong ones can trap water in the wall. The most important is the weep system. Many vinyl and aluminum windows have small slots or holes along the bottom of the frame that let water that gets into the frame drain back out. Caulking over them traps water inside the window and can lead to rot or leaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bottom edge of the exterior trim or frame is another place to think twice. On many installations, the window is flashed so that any water that gets behind the siding drains out at the bottom. Sealing that bottom joint completely can block the drainage path. Follow the window manufacturer's installation guidance and leave intended drainage gaps open.
          </p>

          <p>
            Similarly, do not caulk the gap between a window sash and its frame, or anything that moves when the window opens. That is weatherstripping territory. And on prefinished colored cladding, OSI warns against tooling or smearing Quad Max across the surface, so keep the bead neat and confined to the joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guide to <a href="/where-not-to-caulk/">where not to caulk</a> covers these and other spots around the house in more detail. When in doubt about an unusual joint, check the window maker's installation instructions before you seal it shut.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep-and-application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Prep and Application
          </h2>

          <p>
            Good prep matters more than the brand of sealant. Start by removing the old caulk completely, because new sealant will not bond reliably to old, chalky or failed material. A utility knife, a caulk removal tool and patience get most of it off, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-old-caulk/">removing old caulk</a> covers stubborn residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the joint with a dry brush and wipe it with a cloth dampened in isopropyl alcohol or the cleaner the sealant maker recommends, then let it dry fully. If the joint is deeper than the sealant's limit, 3/8 inch for OSI Quad Max or 1/2 inch for most others, push in backer rod first so the bead stays shallow and bonds only to the two sides.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut the nozzle at an angle to a size just under the gap, and push the bead into the joint rather than dragging it along. Work in sections short enough that you can tool each one before it skins over, which matters with fast-skinning products such as OSI Quad Max. Tool with a caulk tool or a gloved finger to press the sealant into the joint and leave a slightly concave surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Painter's tape on both sides of the joint is the easiest way to a clean line, especially with clear or polymer sealants that are hard to clean up. Pull the tape while the sealant is still wet. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-caulk-exterior-windows/">caulking exterior windows</a> walks through the full process with photos of each stage, and a good <a href="/best-caulk-gun/">caulk gun</a> makes thick sealants far easier to control.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-long-it-lasts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Long Window Caulk Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good exterior sealant, applied properly to a clean, dry joint, typically lasts many years, and the products here are sold on that basis: DAP, for example, backs Alex Fast Dry with a 50-year warranty. In practice, exposure decides most of the lifespan. A bead on a sheltered north-facing window lasts much longer than one on a south-facing window that bakes in the sun.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect window caulk once a year, ideally in late summer before the weather turns. Look for cracks, gaps where the bead has pulled away from the frame or siding, and hard or chalky sections. Small failures can be cut out and patched; widespread cracking means it is time to strip and reseal the whole joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a joint keeps failing, the sealant is often the wrong type or the joint is the wrong shape. A deep, narrow joint filled solid tears early; a wide, shallow bead over backer rod lasts. Moving from an acrylic latex to a rated exterior sealant such as OSI Quad Max is the single most effective upgrade for problem windows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not expect caulk to fix a leaking window by itself. If water is getting in around the frame despite a sound bead, the flashing or the window itself may be the problem. Sealant keeps out weather at the surface; it cannot replace correct installation behind the siding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using interior caulk outside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Basic acrylic latex is easy and cheap, but it has little movement capability and cracks quickly on an exterior window joint. Use a rated exterior sealant such as OSI Quad Max or DAP Dynaflex Ultra for the frame-to-siding joint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking over weep holes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The slots along the bottom of many vinyl and aluminum frames let water drain out. Sealing them traps water inside the window and wall. Leave weep holes and intended drainage gaps open.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking in the cold with a water-based product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                DAP Dynaflex Ultra and Alex Fast Dry need at least 40 degrees. In autumn and winter, choose a low-temperature sealant such as OSI Quad Max or Sashco Lexel, both listed down to 0 degrees.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over old caulk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                New sealant does not bond reliably to old, failed material. Cut and scrape the old bead out, clean the joint and let it dry before applying the new one, or the new bead fails along with the old.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting standard silicone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paint will not stick to ordinary silicone. If the window is painted, choose a paintable product such as GE Paintable Silicone Supreme or a polymer sealant, and wait for the maker's paint-ready time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

