        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best caulk for windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For the exterior frame-to-siding joint, OSI Quad Max is our top pick, rated for plus or minus 50 percent movement and applicable from 0 to 140 degrees, per OSI. For interior trim, DAP Alex Fast Dry is the best choice because it can be brush-painted in about 20 minutes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use silicone or latex caulk around windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Outside, use a rated exterior sealant, which may be silicone, a hybrid or a polymer, rather than basic latex. Inside, a paintable acrylic latex is usually best. Standard silicone cannot be painted, so use a paintable hybrid if the window will be painted.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you caulk windows in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with the right product. OSI Quad Max is listed for application down to 0 degrees and Sashco Lexel down to 0 degrees surface temperature. Water-based products such as DAP Dynaflex Ultra need at least 40 degrees and no frost within 24 hours.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does window caulk take to dry before painting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies by product. DAP Alex Fast Dry can be brush-painted in about 20 minutes, while OSI Quad Max and DAP Dynaflex Ultra are listed as paintable in about an hour. Sashco Lexel needs 24 to 48 hours for latex, and Loctite PL needs a full cure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best clear caulk for windows?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sashco Lexel is our pick for a clear window sealant. Sashco says it is 19 times clearer than silicone and will not yellow, and it can be applied from 0 to 120 degrees. Avoid it near polystyrene foam insulation, which it dissolves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I caulk the bottom of a window?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Be careful. Many windows have weep holes along the bottom of the frame and are flashed to drain water out at the bottom. Sealing those completely can trap water in the wall. Follow the window maker's installation guidance and leave drainage gaps open.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should window caulk be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Inspect it once a year and replace it when it cracks, pulls away or turns hard. A quality exterior sealant applied to a clean, properly shaped joint usually lasts many years, though south-facing windows in full sun wear out sooner.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

